  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 140 AM EDT Thu Aug 13 2026\n\nA welcome (even if brief) respite from rain this morning. Main \nconcern in the immediate near term is potential for some fog \nagain this morning given light winds and ample near-surface \nmoisture. Complicating factor this morning is large area of \nstratus that developed earlier. This stratus is dissipating but \nguidance struggled to capture this and subsequently the modeled \nwidespread, dense fog is very much in question. Will continue to\nmonitor closely through the morning but not seeing enough \nsignals to warrant a dense fog advisory just yet.\n\nUnfortunately, chances for rain return quickly later today and \nespecially tonight as we remain in the \"ring of fire.\" The first \nfeature of interest is an MCV associated with convection presently \nover NE/SD. The feature will track into our CWA during the late \nafternoon with our next chance of rain. However, we continue to see \na step downward in midlevel lapse rates, low level theta-e \nadvection, and subsequent instability (much like yesterday was a step \ndown from the day before). The LLJ is much weaker this morning \n(evidenced by near total lack of convection in our region) and \nsurface obs/mesoanalysis suggest yesterday's activity nudged the \nfront slightly further south. Even seeing some N/NE flow at 850mb \ninto our northern CWA. All of this to say that the afternoon \nactivity is expected to remain weak as it enters our CWA. Some \ngusty winds can't be entirely ruled out though, as well as some \nbrief heavy rain in an area that really can't take anymore.\n\nThe far greater concern will be what happens late tonight/early Fri. \nAnother shortwave ejects into the Central Plains and once again \nstrengthens the downstream LLJ. Another round of strong low level \ntheta-e advection is anticipated, though there is still some spread \nregarding just how strong between the NAM and other hi-res guidance \nvs. lower-res global models. Regardless of the details, the stage is \nset for another round of very heavy rain roughly 04-12Z Fri morning. \nThe exact placement remains somewhat uncertain this far out but a \nconcensus blend would put the highest probabilities squarely over \nthe same area already hit hard in our far southern CWA and \nIND's N/NE CWA. Our flood watch was already extended through Fri\nmorning given this potential and high sensitivity to any rain \nsouth of US-30. An argument could be made to remove the tier of \ncounties right along US-30 but confidence is too low to get \nthat surgical at this point.\n\nThere is some light at the end of this tunnel though. The \neastern Canada upper low keeping our region on the northeast \nfringe of this ridge finally releases eastward allowing the \nridge (and associated storm track) to (temporarily) lift \nnorthward. Storms remain in the forecast for Sat but should be \nmuch more progressive and focused more in our northern CWA vs. \nsouth. The ridge then breaks down completely by late Sunday as a\ncold front crosses the area. Here again, some storms possible \nbut weaker, more progressive and overall just a different setup \nthat is less conducive for heavy rain. Cool and dry conditions \nthen expected Mon through at least early Wed.",
